      "text": "To solve for the measure of \\(\\angle PQR\\), we need to use the information given about the angles and the properties of a straight line.\n\n1. **Identify the given information:**\n   - \\(RSP\\) is a straight line, so the sum of \\(\\angle QSP\\) and \\(\\angle QPR\\) is \\(180^\\circ\\).\n   - \\(\\angle QSP = 80^\\circ\\).\n\n2. **Calculate \\(\\angle QPR\\):**\n   Since \\(\\angle QSP\\) and \\(\\angle QPR\\) are on a straight line, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\angle QPR = 180^\\circ - \\angle QSP = 180^\\circ - 80^\\circ = 100^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the measure of \\(\\angle PQR\\):**\n   To find \\(\\angle PQR\\), we need to consider the sum of the angles in triangle \\(PQR\\). However, we do not have enough information to directly calculate \\(\\angle PQR\\) from \\(\\angle QPR\\) alone. Instead, we can use the fact that the sum of the angles in any triangle is \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\n   Let's denote \\(\\angle PQR\\) as \\(x\\). The sum of the angles in triangle \\(PQR\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\angle QPR + \\angle PQR + \\angle PQS = 180^\\circ.\n   \\]\n   We already know \\(\\angle QPR = 100^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle PQS = 80^\\circ\\) (since \\(\\angle PQS\\) is the same as \\(\\angle QSP\\) because they are corresponding angles when considering the straight line \\(RSP\\)). Therefore, we have:\n   \\[\n   100^\\circ + x + 80^\\circ = 180^\\circ.\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this equation, we get:\n   \\[\n   180^\\circ + x = 180^\\circ.\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(x\\), we find:\n   \\[\n   x = 180^\\circ - 180^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 180^\\circ + 180^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 180^\\circ + 180^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 180^\\circ + 180^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 180^\\circ + 180^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of \\(\\angle PQR\\) is \\(\\boxed{20}\\).",
